(Q4) Explain the difference between hard links and soft links (symbolic
links) in Linux.
Hard Links :
•Same inode number as the original
•No distinction between "original" and "link" – they're all equal references to the same
data
•Cannot cross filesystem boundaries (since inodes are filesystem-specific)
•Cannot link to directories (on most filesystems, for security reasons)
•File data persists as long as at least one hard link exists
Soft Links (Symbolic Links) :
•Different inode from the target file
•Can cross filesystem boundaries
•Can link to directories
•Can point to non-existent files (becomes a "dangling" link)
•Becomes broken if the target file is moved or deleted(Q5) A file has permissions -rwxr-x--x. Explain who can read, write, and
