(Q7) Why is it dangerous to give 777 permissions to a file? Explain with an
example.
Giving 777 permissions (rwxrwxrwx) to a file is dangerous because it grants full read, write, and
execute permissions to everyone—the owner, group members, and all other users on the system.
This creates serious security and stability risks.
•
Anyone can modify the file → Data integrity is lost
•Anyone can read sensitive content → Confidentiality is breached
•Anyone can execute it as a program → Potential for malicious use
•
Malware or compromised accounts can tamper with it → System-wide risk
EXAMPLE (file= passwords.txt)
email: mypass123bank: secure456
You accidentally run:
chmod 777 passwords.txt
Every user on the computer can now:
• Read it → see all your passwords
• Edit it → change or delete your passwords
• Run it (if it were a script) → do harmful things
